---
import PictureMock from './mocks/PictureMock.astro'
---

  <section class="feat rev mock-wide" id="picture">
    <div class="wrap feat-in">
      <div class="feat-copy" data-reveal>
        <h2>The picture, cropped and tuned by eye</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Auto crop finds the black bars baked into a picture, above and below it or at its sides,
          by measuring frames from across the whole file rather than the scene on screen, and the
          window takes the picture's own shape.
          Brightness, contrast, saturation and gamma are sliders the menu clears out of the way of
          while you drag one, so you judge the whole frame rather than what is left around a panel.
        </p>
        <ul class="checks">
          <li><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg>
            <span><b>One choice for the whole season.</b> Pick Auto on one episode and every episode in the folder or torrent is measured for itself as it opens, so a finale shot wider is never cut to the pilot's bars.</span></li>
          <li><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg>
            <span><b>It works on a torrent still downloading</b>, from the stretches that have already arrived.</span></li>
          <li><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg>
            <span><b>A correction for one file, its playlist or everything</b>, and when two of them disagree the player asks which one to keep instead of overwriting either.</span></li>
        </ul>
        <a class="more-link" href="/guides/darker-than-quicktime">Why the same video looks darker in mpv than in QuickTime</a>
      </div>
      <div class="feat-mock" data-reveal style="--dl:1">
        <PictureMock />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
